/**
 * PURPOSE
 *   Contact-page-only ambient background: injects a real, accurately-
 *   coastlined world map (not hand-drawn approximations) as a fixed,
 *   low-opacity decorative layer, weighted to the right side of the
 *   viewport, zoomed in on Iberia with a Google-Maps-style pin over
 *   Braga (northern Portugal). Portugal's own outline is traced by a
 *   brighter, glowing dash that continuously travels around it —
 *   like it's being repainted — while the rest of the coastlines stay
 *   static. Replaces the earlier canvas-drawn Iberian map
 *   (iberian-map.js).
 *
 * RESPONSIBILITIES
 *   - Inject the map markup (below) wrapped in a
 *     <div class="world-map"><svg>...</svg></div>, as the first child
 *     of <body>.
 *   - Do nothing else. All positioning, fade, stroke styling, and the
 *     pin/trace animations live in CSS (assets/css/effects/world-map.css)
 *     since none of it needs per-frame JS — a big simplification over
 *     the previous canvas + requestAnimationFrame approach.
 *
 *   The map markup is inlined as a string (not fetched from
 *   assets/images/world-map.svg) on purpose: fetch() of a local file
 *   is blocked by the browser when this site is opened directly as
 *   file:// (no dev server), which silently left the map blank. Inlining
 *   means it always renders, whether the site is opened straight from
 *   disk or served over http(s). assets/images/world-map.svg is kept
 *   in the repo as the source-of-truth copy — see SAFE EDITS below.
 *
 * DEPENDENCIES
 *   assets/css/effects/world-map.css (all visual styling)
 *
 * SAFE EDITS
 *   Pin sits at (480, 123) — Braga, northern Portugal. To reposition
 *   it, edit the <g class="world-map__interest"> transform below (it
 *   carries the shadow and marker together). The animated outline
 *   directly above it (<path class="world-map__prt-trace">) duplicates
 *   the "prt" country path on purpose so it can be styled/animated
 *   independently — if Portugal's coastline data ever changes, update
 *   both copies together.
 *   To show more/less area, adjust the svg's viewBox (currently
 *   "375 88 155 88", a 2x-zoomed crop centered on Iberia) and scale
 *   .world-map__svg's width in world-map.css by the same ratio. If you
 *   regenerate the coastline data, update both this constant and
 *   assets/images/world-map.svg together so they stay in sync.
 */

